COMPLETE REVISION SHEET

1. ELECTROSTATIC POTENTIAL ENERGY (U)
- Concept: Energy of a system, not a single charge. Conservative nature of electrostatic forces allows energy storage.
- Fundamental Definition: Change in potential energy ΔU = Uf - Ui = -Wint (negative of work done by internal electrostatic forces).
- ⭐ Work Done by External Agent: Wext = ΔU (Condition: ΔKE = 0, moved slowly).
- Potential Energy of 2 Point Charges: U = kq₁q₂/r
- 📌 Assumption: U = 0 at r = ∞.
- 📌 Sign Convention: Use actual signs of charges. (+) U = Repulsive system; (-) U = Attractive (Bound) system.
- System of n Charges: Sum of interaction energies of all pairs (ⁿC₂ pairs).
- 💡 Example (3 charges): Usys = k(q₁q₂/r₁₂ + q₁q₃/r₁₃ + q₂q₃/r₂₃).
- Equilibrium: Systems naturally move toward minimum potential energy states.

2. ELECTRIC POTENTIAL (V)    # derivation h iska, V = W/q
- Concept: Property of a point in space created by source charge. Scalar tool to avoid vector calculations.
- Definition: V = U/q. Work done per unit positive charge to bring it from ∞ to a point.
- Relation between E and V: ΔV = Vf - Vi = -∫ E . dr    # iska derivation @ photo
- 📌 Directional Property: Electric field (E) always points in the direction of decreasing potential.
- Potential due to Point Charge: V = kq/r
# potential is the property of the location
# electric force is conservative, therefore electric potential does not depend on the path it depends only on the initial point (infinity) and final point (that point)

3. POTENTIAL FOR STANDARD GEOMETRIES
- Finite Charged Rod (Length L): V = (kQ/L) ln[(a+L)/a] (a = distance from end).
- ⭐ Ring (Center): V = kQ/R (Distance R is constant for all charge elements).
- ⭐ Ring (Axial Point): V = kQ / √(R² + x²).
- Uniformly Charged Disc (Axial): V = (σ/2ε₀) [√(R² + x²) - x] OR V = (σ/2ε₀) (1 - cosθ) √(R² + x²).
- ⭐ Thin Spherical Shell (Hollow):
  - Inside (r < R): V = kQ/R (Constant everywhere inside).
  - Outside (r ≥ R): V = kQ/r.
- ⭐ Solid Non-Conducting Sphere:
  - Inside (r < R): V = [kQ / (2R³)] (3R² - r²) (The "Chuudail Formula").
  - 📌 Center (r = 0): Vc = 1.5 x Vsurface = 3kQ / 2R.
  - Outside: V = kQ/r.
- Infinite Long Wire: Va - Vb = 2kλ ln(Rb/Ra).

4. ELECTRIC DIPOLE
- Definition: Two equal/opposite charges (±q) separated by small distance d.
- ⭐ Dipole Moment (p): p = q . d (Vector direction: -q to +q).
- Electric Field (Short Dipole):
      - Axial: E = 2kp / r³ (parallel to p).
      - Equatorial: E = -kp / r³ (anti-parallel to p).
      - General Point: E_net = (kp / r³) √ (1 + 3 cos²θ).
- ⭐ Potential due to Dipole: V = (kp cosθ) / r² = k(p . r) / r³.
- Dipole in Uniform External Field:
  - Net Force: Fnet = 0.
  - ⭐ Torque: τ = p x E = pE sinθ.
  - ⭐ Potential Energy: U = -p . E = -pE cosθ.
  - Work in Rotation: Wext = pE (cosθ₁ - cosθ₂).
- 📌 Stable Equilibrium: θ = 0° (p || E). U is min, τ = 0.
- 📌 Unstable Equilibrium: θ = 180° (p anti-|| E). U is max, τ = 0.
- 💡 Oscillation: Small displacement from stable equilibrium leads to Angular SHM.
  - Time Period: T = 2π √ (I / pE) (I = moment of inertia).
- ⚠️ Non-Uniform Field/Interaction: Calculate force using F = -dU/dr after finding U.

5. ELECTROSTATICS OF CONDUCTORS
- Electric Field Inside: E = 0 inside the material.
- Net Charge: Excess charge resides only on the outer surface.
- Potential: Entire conductor (interior + surface) is at the same potential (Equipotential).
- Field Lines: Always hit/leave surface perpendicularly.
- Surface Charge Density (σ): σ ∝ 1/R. Charge accumulates at sharp points (small radius).
- 📌 Connections: When two bodies connect, charge flows until potentials (V) are equal.
- 📌 Concentric Shells: If an inner conductor is connected to an outer one, all inner charge flows to the outer surface.
- ⭐ Parallel Plate Distribution: Q_outer_surface = (Σ Qi) / 2 (Half of total charge on each outermost face).

Properties of equipotential surface -
a) Equipotential surface has same potential at every point.
b) Work done on moving a charge on a equipotential surface from one point to another is zero.
c) Electric field lines are perpendicular to equipotential surface.
d) Two equipotential surfaces do not intersect each other because at one point two different values of potential is not possible.
e) Equipotential surfaces are closer in stronger electric field and farther apart in weak electric field.

6. SELF-POTENTIAL ENERGY (U_self)    # not in boards
- Definition: Energy required to assemble a system from scratch (bringing infinitesimal dq from ∞).
- Point Charge: U_self = 0.
- ⭐ Hollow Shell / Conducting Sphere: U_self = kQ² / 2R = Q² / (8πε₀R).
  - Represents energy stored in E-field from r = R to r = ∞.
- ⭐ Solid Non-Conducting Sphere: U_self = 3/5 (kQ² / R).
  - Energy is higher because charge is packed throughout volume.
- 💡 Rule of Thumb: Use U_self only if the size/shape/geometry of a single body changes.

- ⭐ N-Drops Problem (Merging drops):
  - Charge: Q = Nq.
  - Volume/Radius: R = N^(¹/³) r.
  - Energy Change: ΔU = U_final - (N x U_initial_per_drop).

7. PROBLEM SOLVING TECHNIQUES
- Mechanical Energy Conservation: Use KEi + Ui = KEf + Uf for moving charges.
- 💡 Shell "Setting" Method: Start from innermost charge, place opposite charge on facing surfaces to maintain E = 0 inside material.
- 📌 Earthing: Earthing a conductor makes its potential zero (V = 0), not necessarily its charge.

